A||N||10||bHooper||ok, can someone explain how a starter works?||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-03-2000||09:16 PM||||I am installing my tranny tonight and useing a remote starter to spin the cars starter (thanks George) but flywheel does not engage.  Can someone pleaase explain to me... again... how a starter properly operates?<P>Here's what I think:  A perfectly operating starter, spins, causing a gear to spin out and engage the flywheel, in turn causing it to spin and start the engine.<P>If this gear does not engage the flywheel, then the starter is bad, correct?  When I get a new starter, does it include the gear and selanoid?<P>In the other post, Gokart mentioned a shim... can anyone elaborate.  <P>Thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||bad88||05-03-2000||09:27 PM||||you are correct on the operation of the starter.  What does it sound like when you try to turn over the engine?  If the starter is bad (solenoid is connected to started so its considered part of the starter when purchasing a new one, along with the gear). If you hear the starter spin but not engage it may be that the solenoid is not pushing the gear far enough. If the car just clicks it could be the starter of possibly the voltage regulator (I can't remember if the Fiero has a seperate one or not though.) Anyway make sure the problem is in the starter first before replacing it.  Most Auto parts stores can test the starter for you and that should help you narrow down the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||05-03-2000||09:39 PM||||The starter solinoid serves two purposes:<BR>1.) It acts as a switch between the battery and the starter. When you energize the solinoid, it acts like a relay.<BR>2.) It creates an electromagnetic coil to push the starter drive out to engage the flywheel.<BR>The solinoid on my 85 is going bad- It will engage the starter drive, but not complete the circuit to power up the starter motor. Another piece of crap GM product- a 15 year old starter and it doesnt work every once in a while. I wonder if they will fix it under warrenty?||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||05-03-2000||09:58 PM||||correct, the starter does spin, but the gear does not move.  George (Fiero Shop)tried to explain this to me, but I had to make sure I understood him!<P>I will purchase a new starter tommorow.<P>Thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||05-03-2000||11:58 PM||||bhooper, the solenoid and the starter are normally and last time I looked are togetther and is considered a single unit even though you can purchase a solenoid separatly.<BR>The proceedures as cooter stated are correct. One thing you do not have to worry about is the voltage regulator as it is part of the alternator. One thing that I did not mention to you on the phone is that there should be a spacer between the starter and the engine block. This spacer is a long piece of metal that is a little longer the the bolt holes on the starter. It has a cut out on one end, a hole towards the other end and has what looks like a handle at the end colsest to the hole. It is aprox. 1/2 inch wide and about a 32 of an inch thick. In other words a funny looking long washer that fits between the starter and the engine block. Take out the startre and most likely you will see the spacer, then go and get you another starter.||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||05-04-2000||01:15 PM||||Argh!! Dar!! Duh!! <B>I AM SO PATHETICALLY STUPID!!! DARRRRR!!!!</B>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>So I went to Advance auto to buy a new starter, and while waiting in line to check out... I read the manual.  It says to take a screwdriver and slide the starter gear in place against the flywheel! <P>Why didn't I think of this last night?  I could have had the stupid tranny in last night!! <P><B>ARGH!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!</B><P>Oh well, now I have a new starter to place into the car...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 05-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||cscougar||05-04-2000||03:35 PM||||Can anyone tell me if a 4cyl starter will work on a 6 cyl?  I have a practically new Delco starter on my old 2m4 engine and since I am installing a low mileage 2.8 v-6 engine, I am thinking about transferring the new starter to it.<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||05-04-2000||03:51 PM||||I think there is another active thread on this, and the consensous is it will not work, only quickly burn out||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||05-04-2000||03:53 PM||||... but then again, starters are relatively cheap.  Advanced auto has them for $37||||reg||1
Z||000009||DJRice||05-05-2000||09:31 AM||||They will burn out, I know from experience.  New ones come with a lifetime warranty.  Get a new one.||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||05-05-2000||10:08 AM||||thats all done, as of thursday evening.  the new starter is installed and the tranny is mounted.  i now have a much better understanding of how a starter works.<P>thanks to everyone that offered advice on this subject!<P>hoop||||reg||1
